<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
<meta charset="utf-8" />
<title>Nigeria Conflict StoryMap (1997 - 2025)</title>

<link rel="stylesheet" href="https://unpkg.com/leaflet/dist/leaflet.css" />
<script src="https://unpkg.com/leaflet/dist/leaflet.js"></script>
<script src="https://unpkg.com/scrollama"></script>

<style>
  body, html {
    margin: 0;
    padding: 0;
    font-family: Arial, sans-serif;
    overflow-x: hidden;
    color: #f5efef;
    text-align: justify;
  }

  p {
    line-height: 1.6;
    font-size: 15px;
  }
  /* Two Column Layout */
  .container {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 65% 35%;
    height: 100vh;
    width: 100%;
    position: relative;
  }

  #map {
    width: 100%;
    height: 100%;
    position: sticky;
    top: 0;
    z-index: 1;
  }

  #story {
    padding: 60px 40px;
    overflow-y: scroll;
    height: 100vh;
    box-sizing: border-box;
    background: rgb(58, 57, 57);
  }

  section {
    margin-bottom: 80vh;
  }

  h2, h1 {
    margin-top: 0;
  }

  html {
    scroll-behavior: smooth;
  }

  .legend {
    background: rgb(0, 0, 0);
    padding: 8px 12px;
    border-radius: 5px;
    font-size: 13px;
    line-height: 18px;
    border: 1px solid #999;
  }

  .leaflet-interactive {
    transition: fill-opacity 1s ease, fill 1s ease, stroke 0.8s ease;
  }

  .region-label {
    font-weight: bold;
    color: #111111;
    background: rgba(255,255,255,0.7);
    padding: 2px 5px;
    border-radius: 3px;
    font-size: 12px;
    pointer-events: none;
  }

  .region-img {
    width: 100%;
    max-height: 300px;
    object-fit: cover;
    border-radius: 5px;
    margin-top: 15px;
    box-shadow: 0 2px 6px #ffff;
  }

  .region-figure {
    margin: 20px 0;
    text-align: center;
    color: #ddd;
    font-size: 14px;
  }
  .region-figure figcaption {
    margin-top: 8px;
    font-style: italic;
  }

</style>
</head>

<body>

<div class="container">

  <div id="map"></div>

  <div id="story">

    <section>
      <h1>Conflict Patterns and Security Dynamics in Nigeria (1997 - 2025)</h1>
      <p>Across Nigeria, patterns of violence have unfolded differently from region to region.
        While the causes vary, ranging from; insurgency to communal clashes, banditry, militancy,
        and protest confrontations, the human cost is shared. When we look across the regions, 
        we see different stories, but the same shared consequence: lives interrupted and communities changed.
        The data highlights how these impacts have concentrated differently across Nigeria's geopolitical regions.</p>
      <p><b><i>This storymap examines spatial patterns of conflict events and fatalities using ACLED data aggregated at the 
        state level.</b></i></p>
      
      <figure class="region-figure">
          <img src="https://github.com/Nellchris/Nigeria-Conflict-from-1997-to-2025/raw/main/images/Introduction.jpg" alt="Nigeria Conflict Introduction" class="region-img" />
          <figcaption>Independence Day morning, capturing the essence of sovereignty, cultural pride, and collective strength </figcaption>
      </figure>
      
    </section>

    <section data-region="north_central">
      <h2>North Central Region</h2>
      <p>(Benue, Plateau, Nasarawa, Niger, Kogi, Kwara, Abuja FCT)<br><br>
        This region registers 8,704 conflict events resulting in 24,134 fatalities. The dominant pattern here is
        Violence Against Civilians, driven largely by farmer-herder tensions, land resource disputes, communal boundary
        conflicts, and cycles of reprisal attacks. States such as: Plateau, Benue, and Nasarawa are severe hotspots, where
        land fragmentation, cattle migration corridors, and weak dispute-resolution institutions intensify violence.
        The region's conflicts are highly localized and communal, also often driven by insurgency. 
        The region illustrates how environmental stress, identity, and territory can collide in ways that become deadly.</p>
      
      <figure class="region-figure">
          <img src="https://github.com/Nellchris/Nigeria-Conflict-from-1997-to-2025/raw/main/images/North-Central.jpg" alt="North Central Region Conflict" class="region-img" />
          <figcaption>Whole towns and villages have been destroyed in the conflict (BBC news Updates, May 2016)</figcaption>
      </figure>
    </section>

    <section data-region="north_east">
      <h2>North East Region</h2>
      <p>(Borno, Yobe, Adamawa, Bauchi, Taraba, Gombe)<br><br>
        This region records the highest conflict burden in the country, with 9,173 events and 56,290 fatalities.
        Battles are the dominant event type due to the prolonged Boko Haram and ISWAP insurgency, especially centered
        in Borno and surrounding border regions. Conflict dynamics here reflect territorial contestation, asymmetric
        warfare, and military counter-insurgency campaigns. The violence is ideological and militarized, producing
        long-term displacement and humanitarian crisis conditions. This region's story is one of direct confrontation, 
        where towns became frontlines and survival became a daily negotiation. The devastation has reshaped livelihoods, 
        movement, education, and the very idea of home.</p>
      
      <figure class="region-figure">
        <img src="https://github.com/Nellchris/Nigeria-Conflict-from-1997-to-2025/raw/main/images/North-East.jpg" alt="North East Region Conflict" class="region-img" />
        <figcaption>Children play in a small river created by heavy rains, next to the area where latrines are constructed inside a camp for internally displaced people 
          in Pulka town, northeast Nigeria. May, 2018. © Igor Barbero/MSF</figcaption>
      </figure>
    </section>

    <section data-region="north_west">
      <h2>North West Region</h2>
      <p>(Zamfara, Kaduna, Katsina, Sokoto, Kebbi, Kano, Jigawa)<br><br>
        The North West accounts for 8,746 events and 31,976 fatalities, with Violence Against Civilians being the most
        prevalent. The region is marked by banditry, mass kidnapping, rural terror networks, cattle rustling economies,
        and informal armed militias, particularly concentrated in Zamfara, Kaduna, and Katsina. Unlike the North East,
        the violence here is not driven by ideology, but by criminal economies and rural governance breakdown. 
        Here, The insecurity is diffuse — harder to predict, harder to contain.</p>
      
      <figure class="region-figure">
        <img src="https://github.com/Nellchris/Nigeria-Conflict-from-1997-to-2025/raw/main/images/North-West.jpg" alt="North West Region Conflict" class="region-img" />
        <figcaption>The Other Insurgency: Northwest Nigeria's Worsening Bandit Crisis by James Barnett and Murtala Rufai November 2021</figcaption>
      </figure>
    </section>

    <section data-region="south_west">
      <h2>South West Region</h2>
      <p>(Lagos, Oyo, Osun, Ogun, Ondo, Ekiti)<br><br>
        The South West accounts for 6,215 events but a comparatively lower fatality impact of 4,435. Here, Protests are
        the dominant event type, reflecting urban political mobilization, including #EndSARS in 2020, civic activism, student
        movements, and labor actions. Conflict tends to be episodic and city-centered, rather than rural or insurgent, 
        reflecting the pressures of governance, inequality, and civic expression in major cities.</p>
      
      <figure class="region-figure">
        <img src="https://github.com/Nellchris/Nigeria-Conflict-from-1997-to-2025/raw/main/images/South-West.jpg" alt="South West Region Conflict" class="region-img" />
        <figcaption>People protest against hunger and economic hardships on the street in Lagos, Nigeria, on Friday, August 2024. (Sunday Alamba / Associated Press)</figcaption>
      </figure>
    </section>

    <section data-region="south_south">
      <h2>South South Region</h2>
      <p>(Rivers, Bayelsa, Delta, Akwa Ibom, Cross River, Edo)<br><br>
        With 6,818 events and 9,434 fatalities, the South South's conflicts are tied to resource control disputes, oil bunkering,
        militant operations, and environmental justice struggles. The violence is economic and extractive in nature, linked to oil
        infrastructure sabotage, armed revenue groups, and historical grievances over marginalization. 
        Generally, this region's conflict dynamics are deeply tied to the struggle over resource control, 
        where economic grievances turn into armed expressions of resistance and retaliation</p>

      <figure class="region-figure">
        <img src="https://github.com/Nellchris/Nigeria-Conflict-from-1997-to-2025/raw/main/images/South-South.jpeg" alt="South South Region Conflict" class="region-img" />
        <figcaption>Oil resource rich countries experience varying degrees of resource conflicts. Here, a tanker truck with stolen refined petroleum product was set ablaze in Rivers State, 
          Nigeria (29 January 2022). PHOTO: REUTERS/AFOLABI SOTUNDE</figcaption>
      </figure>
    </section>

    <section data-region="south_east">
      <h2>South East Region</h2>
      <p>(Anambra, Imo, Ebonyi, Enugu, Abia)<br><br>
        The South East records 4,250 events and 5,389 fatalities, with Violence Against Civilians as the leading event type.
        Conflict patterns are linked to secessionist agitation, localized enforcement of “sit-at-home” directives, and confrontations
        between pro-Biafra groups and security forces. Attacks on public institutions, market centers, and political actors highlight
        the region's identity-driven and state legitimacy conflicts. Lastly, Security operations, political agitation, and retaliatory attacks have 
        shaped a cycle of fear, statements, and counter-statements in this region.</p>

      <figure class="region-figure">
        <img src="https://github.com/Nellchris/Nigeria-Conflict-from-1997-to-2025/raw/main/images/South-East.jpeg" alt="South East Region Conflict" class="region-img" />
        <figcaption>Part of the Owerri prison in Imo state attacked on April 4, 2021 and 1,844 inmates released. FILE PHOTO | NIGERIA CORRECTIONAL SERVICES</figcaption>
      </figure>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Conclusion</h2>
      <p>Conflict in Nigeria is not one story, it is many. Nigeria's security challenges are diverse, layered, and regionally distinct. 
        Each region experiences violence differently, shaped by its own history, economy, and social identity. The tragedy is shared, 
        but the drivers and experiences are not uniform. Understanding these regional patterns is essential, not only for recounting what 
        has happened, but for shaping targeted, context-aware responses that support peace, resilience, and community rebuilding.</p>
      
      <figure class="region-figure">
          <img src="https://github.com/Nellchris/Nigeria-Conflict-from-1997-to-2025/raw/main/images/Conclusion.jpeg" alt="Conclusion" class="region-img" />
          <figcaption>Photo of the Nigerian Army</figcaption>
      </figure>
    </section>

  </div>
</div>

<script>
const regions = {
  north_central: ["Benue","Kogi","Kwara","Nasarawa","Niger","Plateau","Abuja FCT"],
  north_east: ["Adamawa","Bauchi","Borno","Gombe","Taraba","Yobe"],
  north_west: ["Jigawa","Kaduna","Kano","Katsina","Kebbi","Sokoto","Zamfara"],
  south_east: ["Abia","Anambra","Ebonyi","Enugu","Imo"],
  south_south: ["Akwa Ibom","Bayelsa","Cross River","Delta","Edo","Rivers"],
  south_west: ["Ekiti","Lagos","Ogun","Ondo","Osun","Oyo"]
};

var map = L.map('map').setView([9.5, 8.0], 6);

L.tileLayer('https://{s}.basemaps.cartocdn.com/dark_all/{z}/{x}/{y}{r}.png', {
  attribution: '&copy; <a href="https://www.openstreetmap.org/copyright">OpenStreetMap</a> contributors &copy; <a href="https://carto.com/attributions">CARTO</a>',
  subdomains: 'abcd',
  maxZoom: 19
}).addTo(map);

let stateLayer;
let labelsLayer = L.layerGroup().addTo(map);

// Styles
function baseStyle() { return { color: "#777", weight: 1, fillOpacity: 0.25 }; }
function highlightStyle() { return { color: "#b30000", weight: 2, fillColor: "#ff6666", fillOpacity: 0.75 }; }

fetch("https://raw.githubusercontent.com/Nellchris/Nigeria-Conflict-from-1997-to-2025/refs/heads/main/State_EF.geojson")
  .then(res => res.json())
  .then(data => {
    stateLayer = L.geoJson(data, {
      style: baseStyle,
      onEachFeature: (feature, layer) =>
        layer.bindTooltip(`<b>${feature.properties.STATE}</b><br>Events: ${feature.properties.EVENTS}<br>Fatalities: ${feature.properties.FATALITIES}`)
    }).addTo(map);

    setupScroll();
    addLegend();
  });

// Focus Region with animated labels
function focusRegion(regionKey) {
  labelsLayer.clearLayers();
  stateLayer.setStyle(f => regions[regionKey]?.includes(f.properties.STATE) ? highlightStyle() : baseStyle());

  const selected = [];
  stateLayer.eachLayer(l => {
    if (regions[regionKey]?.includes(l.feature.properties.STATE)) {
      selected.push(l);

      // Add label at centroid
      const centroid = l.getBounds().getCenter();
      L.marker(centroid, {
        icon: L.divIcon({
          className: 'region-label',
          html: `<b>${l.feature.properties.STATE}</b>`,
          iconSize: [100, 20]
        })
      }).addTo(labelsLayer);
    }
  });

  if (selected.length) map.flyToBounds(L.featureGroup(selected).getBounds(), { padding: [50,50], duration: 2 });
}

// Choropleth for fatalities with smooth fade
function getColor(fatalities) {
  return fatalities > 40000 ? '#800026' :
         fatalities > 10000  ? '#BD0026' :
         fatalities > 5000  ? '#E31A1C' :
         fatalities > 2000  ? '#FC4E2A' :
         fatalities > 1000   ? '#FD8D3C' :
         fatalities > 500   ? '#FEB24C' :
                             '#FFEDA0';
}

let choroplethLegend;

// Create legend (once)
function createChoroplethLegend() {
  if (!choroplethLegend) {
    choroplethLegend = L.control({ position: "bottomright" });
    choroplethLegend.onAdd = () => {
      const div = L.DomUtil.create("div", "legend");
      div.style.opacity = 0; // start hidden
      div.style.transition = "opacity 1s ease";
      div.innerHTML = `
        <b>Fatalities</b><br>
        <i style="background:#800026;width:14px;height:14px;display:inline-block;border:1px solid #555;"></i> >40000<br>
        <i style="background:#BD0026;width:14px;height:14px;display:inline-block;border:1px solid #555;"></i> 10000-40000<br>
        <i style="background:#E31A1C;width:14px;height:14px;display:inline-block;border:1px solid #555;"></i> 5000-10000<br>
        <i style="background:#FC4E2A;width:14px;height:14px;display:inline-block;border:1px solid #555;"></i> 2000-5000<br>
        <i style="background:#FD8D3C;width:14px;height:14px;display:inline-block;border:1px solid #555;"></i> 1000-2000<br>
        <i style="background:#FEB24C;width:14px;height:14px;display:inline-block;border:1px solid #555;"></i> 500-1000<br>
        <i style="background:#FFEDA0;width:14px;height:14px;display:inline-block;border:1px solid #555;"></i> 0-500
      `;
      return div;
    };
  }
}

// Show choropleth with smooth fade-in
function showChoropleth() {
  stateLayer.eachLayer(layer => {
    layer.setStyle({
      fillColor: getColor(layer.feature.properties.FATALITIES),
      fillOpacity: 0,
      color: "#555",
      weight: 1
    });
    // Fade in after a tiny delay
    setTimeout(() => layer.setStyle({ fillOpacity: 0.7 }), 100);
  });

  createChoroplethLegend();
  choroplethLegend.addTo(map);
  setTimeout(() => choroplethLegend.getContainer().style.opacity = 1, 100);
}

// Hide choropleth with smooth fade-out
function hideChoropleth() {
  stateLayer.eachLayer(layer => layer.setStyle({ fillOpacity: 0 }));
  if (choroplethLegend) choroplethLegend.getContainer().style.opacity = 0;

  // After fade-out delay, reset to base style and remove legend
  setTimeout(() => {
    stateLayer.eachLayer(layer => layer.setStyle(baseStyle()));
    if (choroplethLegend) map.removeControl(choroplethLegend);
  }, 1000); // delay matches CSS transition
}

// Scrollama setup for Conclusion section
function setupScroll() {
  const scroller = scrollama();
  scroller.setup({ step: "#story section", offset: 0.6 })
    .onStepEnter(e => {
      const region = e.element.dataset.region;
      const heading = e.element.querySelector('h2')?.innerText;

      if (region) {
        labelsLayer.clearLayers();
        hideChoropleth(); // fade out if scrolling away from conclusion
        focusRegion(region);
      } else if (heading === "Conclusion") {
        labelsLayer.clearLayers();
        showChoropleth(); // fade in choropleth and legend
        map.fitBounds(stateLayer.getBounds(), { padding: [50,50] });
      }
    })
    .onStepExit(e => {
      const heading = e.element.querySelector('h2')?.innerText;
      if (heading === "Conclusion") {
        hideChoropleth(); // fade out with delay
      }
    });
}


</script>

</body>
</html>
